      <h2>Dataset Overview &amp; Historical Context</h2>
    </div>

    <p>The Academy Award for Best Original Score has been conferred at every ceremony since 1934, spanning 93 consecutive years through the 98th Academy Awards in March 2026. Over that time, <strong>79 individual composers</strong> have claimed the honor, scoring <strong>92 unique films</strong> (with <em>Oppenheimer</em> appearing in both the 2023 and 2024 data entries for Ludwig G&ouml;ransson). The category has evolved&mdash;splitting at times into dramatic and comedy/musical subcategories&mdash;but this dataset consolidates to one winner per ceremony year, providing a clean longitudinal record.</p>

    <p>What emerges from nine decades of data is a category defined by rare, concentrated brilliance. Nearly three-quarters of all winners (62 of 79 individual composers) took the prize exactly once, while a small elite returned to the podium again and again. The interplay between these one-time victors and serial champions is the central tension of this history&mdash;and the story this report sets out to tell.</p>

