  <div class="section" id="era-analysis">
    <div class="section-header">
      <div class="section-number">4</div>
      <h2>Era-Based Analysis of Winning Scores</h2>
    </div>

    <p>Breaking the 93-year history into four broad eras reveals distinct compositional identities and patterns of dominance. The boundaries below follow conventional film-music historiography, though the transitions between eras are gradual rather than abrupt.</p>

    <div class="era-grid">

      <div class="era-card golden">
        <h3>The Golden Age</h3>
        <div class="era-years">1934&ndash;1959 &middot; 26 ceremonies</div>
        <span class="era-stat">25 unique composers</span>
        <span class="era-stat">Dominated by European &eacute;migr&eacute;s</span>
        <p>The founding era of the Oscar score was shaped by composers who had emigrated from Europe&rsquo;s conservatories to Hollywood. <strong>Max Steiner</strong> (3 wins), <strong>Dimitri Tiomkin</strong> (3 wins), and <strong>Franz Waxman</strong> (2 consecutive wins, 1950&ndash;51) defined a lush, orchestral style rooted in late-Romantic tradition. The era also featured notable collaborative credits: the 1939 <em>Stagecoach</em> win was shared among four composers, and Disney&rsquo;s animated features (<em>Pinocchio</em>, <em>Dumbo</em>) brought multi-composer teams to the podium. Despite these repeat winners, the era maintained healthy diversity&mdash;25 unique composers across 26 ceremonies.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="era-card silver">
        <h3>The Silver Age</h3>
        <div class="era-years">1960&ndash;1979 &middot; 20 ceremonies</div>
        <span class="era-stat">18 unique composers</span>
        <span class="era-stat">Epic &amp; genre diversification</span>
        <p>The Silver Age saw film scoring broaden beyond the symphonic mold. <strong>Maurice Jarre</strong> brought sweeping, geographically inflected scores to David Lean epics (2 of his 3 career wins fall here). <strong>John Barry</strong> emerged with two wins (1966, 1968), and <strong>John Williams</strong> closed the era with twin blockbuster triumphs: <em>Jaws</em> (1975) and <em>Star Wars</em> (1977). Pop and jazz infiltrated the category&mdash;Burt Bacharach&rsquo;s <em>Butch Cassidy</em> win (1969) and Giorgio Moroder&rsquo;s electronic <em>Midnight Express</em> score (1978) signaled the Academy&rsquo;s willingness to evolve.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="era-card modern">
        <h3>The Modern Era</h3>
        <div class="era-years">1980&ndash;1999 &middot; 20 ceremonies</div>
        <span class="era-stat">18 unique composers</span>
        <span class="era-stat">Most diverse decade: 1980s (12 composers)</span>
        <p>The Modern Era is defined by its extremes. The 1980s were the most stylistically eclectic decade in Oscar history: no credited composer won twice, and winners ranged from Vangelis&rsquo;s synthesizer-driven <em>Chariots of Fire</em> to Herbie Hancock&rsquo;s jazz score for <em>Round Midnight</em> to the cross-cultural collaboration of Sakamoto, Byrne, and Su on <em>The Last Emperor</em>. Then the 1990s concentrated around two forces: <strong>Alan Menken</strong>&rsquo;s Disney trilogy (3 wins in 4 years, 1989&ndash;1992) and <strong>John Williams</strong>&rsquo;s dramatic pivot with <em>Schindler&rsquo;s List</em> (1993). Hans Zimmer entered the record with <em>The Lion King</em> (1994).</p>
      </div>

      <div class="era-card contemporary">
        <h3>The Contemporary Era</h3>
        <div class="era-years">2000&ndash;2026 &middot; 27 ceremonies</div>
        <span class="era-stat">22 unique composers</span>
        <span class="era-stat">Most globally diverse</span>
        <p>The 21st century has been marked by unprecedented global diversity in winning composers&mdash;Tan Dun (China), Gustavo Santaolalla (Argentina), A.R. Rahman (India), Ludovic Bource (France), and Volker Bertelmann (Germany) all claimed wins. <strong>Ludwig G&ouml;ransson</strong> (Sweden/US) has emerged as the era&rsquo;s dominant figure with 4 wins, including the first back-to-back victories since Santaolalla in 2005&ndash;06. <strong>Hildur Gu&eth;nad&oacute;ttir</strong>&rsquo;s win for <em>Joker</em> (2019) marked a landmark for women in the category. Electronic and hybrid scoring gained further legitimacy through Trent Reznor and Atticus Ross (2 wins).</p>
      </div>

    </div>

