        <h4>1. Extreme Concentration at the Top</h4>
        <p>Seven composers with 3+ wins account for 25 of 93 ceremonies (26.9%). Film scoring&rsquo;s highest honor has always been dominated by a small elite, even as the overall winner pool has grown more diverse.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="takeaway-box">
        <h4>2. Cyclical Diversity</h4>
        <p>The category oscillates between decades of concentrated dominance (1950s: 7 unique composers) and wide-open fields (1980s, 1940s: 12 each). The 2010s returned to high diversity (10 unique composers, only 1 credited repeat), but the 2020s show early signs of reconcentration around G&ouml;ransson.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="takeaway-box">
        <h4>3. The G&ouml;ransson Phenomenon</h4>
        <p>Ludwig G&ouml;ransson has reached 4 wins faster than any predecessor&mdash;8 years vs. Barry&rsquo;s 24 and Williams&rsquo;s 18. At pace, he is positioned to become the solo record-holder before the decade closes, potentially reshaping the all-time leaderboard.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="takeaway-box">
        <h4>4. Globalization of the Podium</h4>
        <p>The Contemporary Era has featured winners from China, Argentina, India, Iceland, France, Germany, and Sweden. The European-&eacute;migr&eacute; pipeline that defined the Golden Age has given way to a truly global talent pool, reflecting cinema&rsquo;s own internationalization.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="takeaway-box full-width">
        <h4>5. Looking Ahead</h4>
        <p>With four remaining ceremonies in the 2020s, the decade&rsquo;s final shape is still being written. Key questions: Will G&ouml;ransson extend his lead? Will the 2020s&rsquo; diversity metrics recover, or will they cement a new era of individual dominance? And will the Academy continue to embrace non-traditional scoring approaches&mdash;electronic, hybrid, and minimalist&mdash;that have gained ground since the 2010s? The data suggests the category is at an inflection point between its historical pattern of cyclical renewal and a potential new age of sustained individual supremacy.</p>
